Trusses, frames and interacting machine members (@mech-members: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mechanical-engineering, #source "../sources/Engineering-Statics-Baker-and-Haynes.lingua#r_3DXGKGQSPPA9YDNH2WSB8XRN80", #mechanical-engineering "Centres-of-mass-centroids-and-distributed-weight.lingua#r_1FWP6BCNJ5094KPFT4SZ16Z84Q") { r_75BWC5200SN37PSBH35JF73XMR
**Read:** [Engineering Statics — Baker and Haynes](../sources/Engineering-Statics-Baker-and-Haynes.lingua#r_3DXGKGQSPPA9YDNH2WSB8XRN80), chapter 6 in full. Work both joint/section methods for ideal trusses and the separate frame/machine examples.

A two-force member has forces at only two points; in static equilibrium those forces must be equal, opposite and collinear. This conclusion follows from the model's loading, not from a part's slender appearance. A pin-jointed truss with joint-applied loads differs from a frame whose members carry transverse loads and moments.

**Work:** Analyze an ideal triangular truss by joints and by a section cut, then compare results. Add a transverse load midway along one member and explain why the original two-force assumption no longer applies. For a simple clamp or linkage, isolate each member and carry connection forces consistently between diagrams.

Check the whole assembly after solving its parts. Internal connection forces must cancel from the assembly balance. Investigate sensitivity to a nearly collinear geometry or changing lever arm; a mechanism that approaches a singular configuration can create large internal forces even with modest applied load.

**Return:** [Mechanical engineering and machine design](../Mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design.lingua#r_0017YZ63HZBZ4P4WD8DECVWE4R).
